<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/advanced/urban-design-and-public-space">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Urban design is <strong>architecture at the scale of behaviour</strong> — streets and squares
    choreograph encounter, commerce and movement. Its founding insight (Jacobs): cities work
    bottom-up from street life, and design that ignores how people actually use space fails
    measurably.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the public realm, the Jacobs revolution, sprawl tools, the 15-minute debate" widget="equationStep" config={urbanCfg} />

  <h2>The evidence-based tools</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sticks and stones of street design:</strong> block length (shorter = more routes), active frontages (doors and windows, not blank walls), street trees (shade, retail uplift ~10%, crash reduction), lane width (narrower = slower = safer — traffic calming is geometry, not signage).</li>
    <li><strong>The plaza experiment:</strong> Whyte\'s time-lapse studies (1980) found people sit where there is sitting — the foundational "observe behaviour, then design" study (the ethnography lesson\'s method, in the square).</li>
    <li><strong>Heat, water and climate adaptation:</strong> urban heat islands (paved cities +5–10 °C — the geography lesson), floodable parks, shade equity (tree canopy maps track income history) — climate adaptation is now urban design\'s central brief (the tipping lesson\'s adaptation portfolio, street by street).</li>
    <li><strong>Tactical urbanism:</strong> paint, planters and pilot closures test changes cheaply before concrete — the A/B lesson\'s experimentation culture, applied to streets; New York\'s pedestrian Times Plaza started as lawn chairs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Jacobs, The Death and Life of Great American Cities (1961); Gehl, Life Between Buildings; Whyte, The Social Life of Small Urban Spaces (1980); the geography ladder\'s urban lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
